---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'

const composeEnv = `services:
  mcpd:
    image: ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd
    ports: ['4317:4317']
    volumes: ['mcpd-data:/data']
    environment:
      MCPD_LISTEN: 0.0.0.0:4317
      MCPD_DATA: /data/mcpd.db
      MCPD_PROJECT: checkout
      MCPD_RETENTION_DAYS: '30'
      MCPD_INGEST_KEYS: write-key-one,write-key-two
      MCPD_READ_KEYS: read-key
      MCPD_REDACT_KEY: a-long-random-string
      MCPD_LOG_FORMAT: json
      MCPD_LOG: mcpd=info,mcpd_server=warn

volumes:
  mcpd-data:`
---

<Doc
  title="Configuration"
  description="Every mcpd flag and environment variable, with its default: listen address, data path, project, retention, ingest and read keys, sampling, redaction, allowed origins, the request deadline and logging."
  lede="Every setting is one flag, and every flag has an environment variable with the same meaning. The few that are too structured for a command line live in one JSON file. This page is all of them."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Two ways to say the same thing</h2>
  <p>
    Run mcpd with no arguments and it works: it listens on
    <code>0.0.0.0:4317</code>, keeps data in <code>./mcpd.db</code>, redacts
    secrets, and stores every trace forever. Everything below changes one of
    those decisions.
  </p>
  <p>
    A flag always wins over its environment variable. Both are global, so they
    are accepted before or after a subcommand.
  </p>

