  <h3>--listen</h3>
  <p>
    The address to bind. One port serves the OTLP endpoints, the native ingest
    endpoint, the query API, the probes, the metrics and the interface, so this
    is the only address mcpd has.
  </p>
  <p>
    Binding a loopback address is the quiet choice: mcpd only warns about open
    ingest when the address it bound is not loopback. On
    <code>0.0.0.0</code> with no keys set, it warns twice at startup, once for
    ingest and once for reading.
  </p>

  <h3>--data</h3>
  <p>
    Path to the SQLite file. Parent directories are created if they do not
    exist. Pass <code>:memory:</code> for a store that leaves nothing behind,
    which is right for a demo or a test and wrong for anything you want to look
    at tomorrow.
  </p>

  <h3>--project</h3>
  <p>
    The project a signal lands in when the sender did not name one. A project is
    the tenancy boundary: every query is scoped to one. The retention pass runs
    against it and against every project in the <code>projects</code> block; a
    project that appears in neither place is never trimmed, which matters if
    your senders name their own.
  </p>

  <h3>--retention-days</h3>
  <p>
    Delete signals older than this many days. Unset keeps everything, which is
    the correct default for a laptop and a decision worth making deliberately
    anywhere else.
  </p>
  <p>
    The window is from 1 to 36500 days, here and in a project's
    <code>retention_days</code>. Zero is refused at start-up rather than read as
    no retention: it would set the cutoff to now, and the first pass runs at
    boot, so it would delete every signal the project holds. To keep everything,
    leave the setting out.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pass runs on an hourly timer rather than at query time, so a large
    delete never lands inside a user's request. Spans, traces, logs and metrics
    are cut by when mcpd received them; scores, feedback and aspect notes by
    their own timestamp. Deleting rows does not shrink the file. See
    <a href="/docs/operations" class="link">Running it</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>--ingest-key, --read-key, --write-key, --admin-key, --execute-key and --worker-key</h3>
  <p>
    Six scopes, because they carry genuinely different risk. Every endpoint
    declares the one it needs in <code>spec/mcpd.spec.json</code>, its handler is
    tagged to match, and <code>make check</code> compares the two, so a scope
    documented here is the scope the running server enforces.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Scope</th>
          <th>Set with</th>
          <th>What it opens</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>ingest</code></td>
          <td><code>--ingest-key</code></td>
          <td>The four ingest endpoints. Reads nothing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>read</code></td>
          <td><code>--read-key</code></td>
          <td>The query API: traces, spans, logs, metrics, scores, analytics.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>write</code></td>
          <td><code>--write-key</code></td>
          <td>
            Scores, feedback, datasets, experiments, acknowledgements, and
            capturing a run into a dataset.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>admin</code></td>
          <td><code>--admin-key</code></td>
          <td>Deleting a trace, reading the audit log, and everything the two scopes below open.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>execute</code></td>
          <td><code>--execute-key</code></td>
          <td>Starting, cancelling and signalling a run, and publishing an event to one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>worker</code></td>
          <td><code>--worker-key</code></td>
          <td>Registering definitions, claiming steps and reporting their outcome. Reads no trace.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The split that matters most is <code>ingest</code> against everything else.
    An ingest key lives in every process that emits a span, including the ones
    running code you did not write. Handing that population a credential that
    can also read back every stored prompt is a mistake mcpd does not make easy:
    an ingest key presented to the query API is refused.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>write</code> is separate from <code>ingest</code> for the same reason
    at a smaller scale. Capturing a run into a dataset reads that run's input and
    output, so it is a read wearing a write's clothes and does not belong on the
    key your fleet carries.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>admin</code> is separate from <code>read</code> because deleting a run
    is not reading one. Until you set <code>--admin-key</code>, a read key
    deletes as well as reads, and mcpd says so at startup.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>execute</code> and <code>worker</code> are the two scopes of the run
    surface. Starting a run makes somebody's worker do work and spend money,
    which annotating a trace does not, so it is not <code>write</code>. A worker
    key lives in the process that runs step bodies, which is usually the process
    holding the provider credential, so it opens the lease protocol and nothing
    that reads a stored prompt. Until you set the two flags, the write keys are
    accepted for both, and an admin key always is.
  </p>
  <p>
    With no <code>--write-key</code> either, the write keys are the ingest keys,
    so the key in every container that emits a span can also start runs and
    claim steps, and a claimed step carries its rendered input. That is the
    shape a deployment configured with only an ingest key and a read key has.
    mcpd says so in a warning at startup and in <code>mcpd doctor</code>, and
    <code>mcpd config</code> prints <code>key required (ingest keys)</code> on
    both lines. Set <code>--execute-key</code> and <code>--worker-key</code> to
    close it.
  </p>

  <h4>Upgrading without an outage</h4>
  <p>
    An unset <code>--write-key</code> accepts the ingest keys, and an unset
    <code>--admin-key</code> accepts the read keys. A deployment configured
    before these two scopes existed therefore keeps exactly the reach it had, and
    nothing breaks on upgrade. Set the two flags to take the narrower grants,
    and <code>mcpd config</code> tells you which state you are in: it prints
    <code>key required</code> when the scope has its own keys and
    <code>key required (ingest keys)</code> or
    <code>key required (read keys)</code> when it is inheriting them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Repeat any of the six flags for several keys, which is how you rotate one
    without an outage. The environment variables take a comma-separated list. A
    caller presents a key as <code>Authorization: Bearer KEY</code> or as
    <code>X-Mcpd-Key: KEY</code>, and the comparison is constant-time so
    neither the length nor the position of the first difference is observable.
  </p>
  <p>
    A key mcpd does not know is a <code>401</code>. A key mcpd knows, used on an
    endpoint its scope does not cover, is a <code>403</code>. The two are worth
    keeping apart: a 401 sends you hunting for a typo in a key that is perfectly
    correct.
  </p>
  <p>
    An empty entry beside a real key is dropped before anything reads the list,
    so a trailing comma adds nothing. A list that is set but holds no key at
    all, such as <code>MCPD_EXECUTE_KEYS=</code> or an empty value in a
    Kubernetes Secret, stops the daemon at start, and the refusal names the flag
    and the variable. Leave the variable out to leave the scope unset. A request
    that sends an empty key header presents no key.
  </p>
  <p>
    A key that is still a placeholder from one of the example files in
    <code>deploy/</code>, any value starting <code>REPLACE_WITH</code> or
    <code>replace-me</code> in any case, stops the daemon starting. The refusal
    names the flag or the project list it is in and never the value. The same
    applies to <code>--redact-key</code>. Generate each key with
    <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    With no keys configured at all, every scope is open. That is deliberate,
    because the first run must not require a credential, and it is why the
    startup warning exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>/healthz</code>, <code>/readyz</code>, <code>/metrics</code> and the
    interface itself are served without a key. Put them behind your own ingress
    if that matters to you.
  </p>

  <h4>The audit log</h4>
  <p>
    The handlers that hand payloads back record who read what:
    <code>{`GET /v1/api/traces/{id}`}</code>, the span and log searches,
    feedback, dataset items, capture, a trace's aspect notes, a theme and its
    examples, delete and erasure.
    <a href="/docs/security" class="link">Security</a> names all eleven
    actions. A request answered with <code>400</code> leaves no row, because
    nothing was read. Each row carries the time, a
    short digest of the key that acted, the action, the project, the resource and
    whether authorisation allowed it. The key itself is never written down.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the log with <code>GET /v1/api/audit</code>, which takes the admin
    scope. It is not trimmed by <code>--retention-days</code>: a log that deleted
    itself on the same schedule as the data it describes could not say who read
    the data that has since gone. Writing a row never fails the read it
    describes; a row that cannot be written is logged and counted as
    <code>mcpd_audit_write_failures_total</code> on <code>/metrics</code>, which
    is worth an alert.
  </p>

  <h3>--sample</h3>
  <p>
    A fraction between 0.0 and 1.0. A value outside that range is a startup
    error, not a silent clamp: <code>--sample must be between 0.0 and 1.0, got
    2</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The decision is made by hashing the trace id, so a fleet of mcpd instances
    behind a load balancer keeps and drops the same traces without talking to
    each other. It is made once per trace and inherited by every span of that
    trace in the batch, because a half-recorded trace looks like a bug in the
    system you are observing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hash reads the trailing bytes of the trace id. Some producers derive the
    leading bytes from a timestamp, and a sampler that looked at those would
    correlate with time of day and quietly drop whole hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    mcpd makes the decision at the head, when the trace starts.
    <a href="/docs/sampling" class="link">Sampling</a> covers head and tail
    decisions.
  </p>

  <h3>--no-redact, --redact-key, --redact-key-contains and --redact-max-string</h3>
  <p>
    Redaction is on by default and runs before anything is written, so a value
    that should never have been persisted never is. Deleting it afterwards is
    not the same guarantee. Three rule sets ship built in, matched on lowercase
    substrings of a key name:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Rule</th>
          <th>Applied when the key contains</th>
          <th>What is stored</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Mask</td>
          <td>
            <code>api_key</code>, <code>apikey</code>, <code>api-key</code>,
            <code>secret</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>passwd</code>,
            <code>access_token</code>, <code>refresh_token</code>,
            <code>id_token</code>, <code>bearer</code>,
            <code>authorization</code>, <code>auth_token</code>,
            <code>private_key</code>, <code>client_secret</code>,
            <code>session_token</code>, <code>credential</code>,
            <code>signature</code>, <code>x-api-key</code>
          </td>
          <td><code>[redacted]</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Partial</td>
          <td>
            <code>email</code>, <code>phone</code>, <code>ssn</code>,
            <code>credit_card</code>, <code>card_number</code>,
            <code>iban</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            The first two and last two characters, <code>ab…yz</code>. A value
            of six characters or fewer is masked entirely rather than leaking
            most of itself
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hash</td>
          <td><code>user_id</code>, <code>userid</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>sha256:</code> and 12 bytes of a keyed digest, so you can
            still group by a user without storing one
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A matched key holding an object or an array has everything underneath it
    redacted too, so <code>{'{'}"credentials": {'{'}"token": "..."{'}'}{'}'}</code>
    does not slip through on the nested name alone. Any single string longer
    than 128 KiB is truncated with a marker naming the byte count, which bounds
    what a runaway prompt does to your disk.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--redact-key</code> is the key for the hashing rule. Equal inputs hash
    equally under one key, which is what makes grouping work, and rotating the
    key breaks that link deliberately. Set it in any deployment that hashes:
    the built-in default is in the source, so unkeyed digests are guessable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--redact-key-contains</code> adds a masking rule of your own on top
    of the built-in three. The built-in rules know the names credentials are
    usually stored under; they do not know the field your own tools put a
    customer's account number in. The value is matched the same way the
    built-in rules are, as a lowercase substring of the key, so
    <code>--redact-key-contains ticket</code> masks <code>ticket_id</code>
    and <code>supportTicket</code> alike, and everything nested under a
    matched key with them. Repeat the flag for several, or set
    <code>MCPD_REDACT_KEY_CONTAINS</code> to a comma-separated list.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--redact-max-string</code> lowers the length cap from its built-in
    128 KiB. A string longer than the cap is cut there, with a marker naming
    how many bytes were dropped, before it is stored.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--no-redact</code> turns the whole pipeline off, including the length
    cap. As an environment variable it is
    <code>MCPD_NO_REDACT=true</code>. It is only sensible when something
    upstream already redacts, and the daemon refuses to start if either
    addition above is set beside it, because there is nothing for them to
    apply to.
  </p>

  <h3>--runs-data</h3>
  <p>
    Where the run store lives: the definitions, the journal of step attempts,
    every deadline and every wait. A file path keeps them in an embedded SQLite
    file, whatever the signal store is. By default it is the
    <code>--data</code> path with <code>.runs</code> before the extension,
    <code>./mcpd.runs.db</code> for the default, and <code>:memory:</code> when
    <code>--data</code> is. <code>--runs-data off</code> serves no runs at all:
    every run endpoint answers <code>503</code> naming this flag, and the rest of
    the daemon is unchanged.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Postgres connection string keeps the runs in Postgres instead, such as
    <code>--runs-data postgres://mcpd:secret@db:5432/mcpd</code>. The string
    starts with <code>postgres://</code> or <code>postgresql://</code>, and it
    reads TLS settings the way <code>--postgres-url</code> does. It can name the
    same database as <code>--postgres-url</code>: the run tables have names of
    their own, and the schema is created on start under an advisory lock.
    Several replicas can share one run store. Every run store transaction is
    <code>SERIALIZABLE</code>, so two replicas claiming, completing or firing
    deadlines at once give the same result as one daemon doing it in turn, and
    a transaction that loses to a concurrent one is run again.
    <code>mcpd config</code> prints the line as <code>postgres at</code> the
    server, with the password removed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The store's timings are in the config file under <code>runs</code>, in whole
    milliseconds: <code>lease_ttl_ms</code>, <code>reclaim_window_ms</code>,
    <code>stall_window_ms</code>, <code>idempotency_window_ms</code>,
    <code>park_ttl_ms</code> and <code>max_depth</code>. The defaults, a 30 second
    lease and a 30 second reclaim window, are right for one daemon on one
    machine and are what a worker reads back when it registers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same block holds <code>max_step_repeats</code>, which is a count rather
    than a duration: how many times one step key may fail with an identical
    rendered input before the run is ended as a repetition. It defaults to 16,
    and zero turns it off. It exists because two retry paths spend no retry
    budget, so <code>retry.max_attempts</code> does not bound them; see
    <a href="/docs/runs" class="link">Runs</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>--cors-origin</h3>
  <p>
    Which browser origins may call the query API from another site. With none
    set, a cross-origin request gets no
    <code>access-control-allow-origin</code> header and the browser refuses to
    hand the page the response. That is the default, and it is what keeps a
    daemon on your laptop with an open read API from being readable by any
    web page open in the same browser. The bundled interface is served from the
    daemon's own origin and never needs this flag.
  </p>
  <p>
    Serving the interface from somewhere else, a CDN or an internal portal,
    is the case this flag exists for: name that origin, as scheme and host,
    such as <code>--cors-origin https://console.example</code>. Repeat it for
    several, or set <code>MCPD_CORS_ORIGINS</code> to a comma-separated list. A
    value that is not an origin, <code>console.example</code> or one with a
    trailing slash, refuses the start with the flag named rather than leaving
    you with an interface that cannot connect and no reason why.
    <code>--cors-origin '*'</code> allows every origin, which is what earlier
    builds did without asking.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>/healthz</code>, <code>/readyz</code> and <code>/metrics</code> are
    not governed by this flag. They carry no stored data, and the interface
    probes <code>/healthz</code> before it does anything else, so they stay
    readable from any origin.
  </p>

  <h3>--request-timeout-seconds</h3>
  <p>
    The longest a request may take, reading the body included, before the
    daemon answers for it. A handler that runs past the deadline is answered
    with a <code>503</code> whose body carries <code>timeout</code> and this
    flag's name. The default is 30 seconds, which is long enough for a large
    batch to be read and stored, and short enough that a storage backend that
    accepts connections and never answers is reported rather than waited on.
    It cannot be turned off; raise it if an operation genuinely needs longer.
  </p>

  <h3>--max-body-bytes and --max-batch-signals</h3>
  <p>
    The two bounds on one request. <code>--max-body-bytes</code> is the largest
    body the daemon reads, 32 MiB by default, and it is what decides how much
    memory one request can take before any of it has been decoded. A larger
    body is refused with <code>413</code> naming both numbers, and a compressed
    one is held to the same figure after it has been inflated, so a body that
    expands a thousandfold is refused rather than expanded.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--max-batch-signals</code> is how many signals one request may carry
    once it has been decoded, 10,000 by default. It is counted across the whole
    request, so an array of batches and an NDJSON stream are held to the same
    number as a single batch. Nothing is stored when a request is over it: the
    refusal comes before the first write rather than after half of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both are also config file keys, <code>max_body_bytes</code> and
    <code>max_batch_signals</code>, and the flag wins where both are set.
    Neither can be turned off, and zero is refused at start-up rather than read
    as "no limit": a body cap of zero refuses every request, so the reading
    that looks generous is the one that takes ingest down.
  </p>

  <h3>--price-book</h3>
  <p>
    A JSON price book to cost model calls with, in place of the built-in one.
    With <code>"extends": "builtin"</code> it is laid over the built-in book and
    needs only the rates that differ. It is also the config file key
    <code>price_book</code>, and the flag wins where both are set. A book with a
    negative rate, tiers out of order or a currency that is not an ISO 4217
    code stops the daemon at start, naming the entry.
    <code>mcpd prices refresh</code> writes one from a public model catalogue.
    See <a href="/docs/cost" class="link">Cost</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>--log-format and MCPD_LOG</h3>
  <p>
    <code>text</code> for a terminal, <code>json</code> for a log pipeline.
    <code>MCPD_LOG</code> takes a <code>tracing</code> filter string and has no
    flag, because it is a debugging control rather than a deployment decision.
    Set it to <code>mcpd_server=debug</code> when you want to see per-request
    detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the two request limits apply</h2>
  <p>
    The body cap applies to every route that reads a body, the query API
    included. The signal cap applies to the ingest doors, and this is what it
    counts at each of them.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>What the signal cap counts</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /v1/ingest</code></td>
          <td>Every span, log, metric, score, piece of feedback and aspect note in the request, added up across all of it, whether it arrived as one batch, an array of batches or a stream of NDJSON lines</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /v1/traces</code></td>
          <td>The spans the sender sent, counted before they are mapped, which is the number the sender can act on</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /v1/logs</code></td>
          <td>The log records in the export</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /v1/metrics</code></td>
          <td>The data points in the export, across every metric in it</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Subcommands</h2>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Command</th>
          <th>What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd serve</code></td>
          <td>Run the daemon. This is what happens with no subcommand at all</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd demo</code></td>
          <td>Write a realistic sample run into the store, then serve, so the interface has something in it the first time you open it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd config</code></td>
          <td>Print the configuration this invocation would use, then exit</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd mcp</code></td>
          <td>Serve the Model Context Protocol over stdio, so an agent can read its own traces from a running daemon</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd doctor</code></td>
          <td>Grade this deployment and report what is misconfigured. Exits 2 on a fault, so it can gate a deploy. See <a href="/docs/operations" class="link">Running it</a></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd relay</code></td>
          <td>Run as an edge collector: accept every ingest request, spool it, and ship it to the daemons <code>--upstream</code> names, in order. Opens no store. See <a href="/docs/relay" class="link">Relay and regions</a></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>mcpd config</code> exists for the argument you are about to have with
    a container. Flags, environment variables and defaults all end up in one
    place, and this prints that place without starting a server or touching the
    data file:
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="mcpd config"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'MCPD_INGEST_KEYS=k1,k2 mcpd --retention-days 30 --sample 0.1 config' },
      { out: 'listen          0.0.0.0:4317' },
      { out: 'storage         sqlite at ./mcpd.db' },
      { out: 'config file     none' },
      { out: 'log format      text' },
      { out: 'project         default' },
      { out: 'projects        none' },
      { out: 'retention       30 days' },
      { out: 'ingest auth     key required', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'read auth       open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'write auth      key required (ingest keys)', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'admin auth      open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'execute auth    key required (ingest keys)', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'worker auth     key required (ingest keys)', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'runs            sqlite at ./mcpd.runs.db' },
      { out: 'run settings    lease_ttl_ms 30000, reclaim_window_ms 30000, stall_window_ms 300000, idempotency_window_ms 86400000, park_ttl_ms 86400000, max_depth 8, max_step_repeats 16' },
      { out: 'identity        off' },
      { out: 'sampling        deterministic 0.1' },
      { out: 'quota           unlimited' },
      { out: 'redaction       on', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'redact key      built in, which is public; set --redact-key', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'cors            same-origin only' },
      { out: 'timeout         30s per request' },
      { out: 'limits          32 MiB per request, 10000 signals per batch' },
      { out: 'prices          builtin, 1365 rates in USD, as of 2026-10-12' },
      { out: 'derived         duration and token metrics' },
      { out: 'aspects         off' },
      { out: 'monitors        none' },
      { out: 'objectives      none' },
      { out: 'judge           off' },
      { out: 'forwarding      off' },
      { out: 'relay           off: no --upstream' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Read the six auth lines before you deploy anything. <code>open</code> means
    open to whoever can reach the port. A line in brackets names the wider list
    that scope is inheriting from, which is the state a deployment upgraded from
    an older release starts in. Every flag and every config file key has a line
    here, with the value the daemon runs rather than the one written: an
    interval under its floor is printed raised, with the number it was raised
    from. Nothing secret is printed. Keys are counted, never listed, a password
    is <code>set</code> or <code>unset</code>, a forward header is printed by
    name, and a URL or a connection string is printed without its credentials.
  </p>

  <h2>A configured deployment</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing here is mcpd-specific: the variables are ordinary environment, so
    they work the same in Compose, in a Kubernetes manifest and in a systemd
    unit.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="compose"
    samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'docker-compose.yml', shiki: 'yaml', code: composeEnv }]}
  />

  <p>
    The same settings as flags, for a shell or a unit file:
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    command="mcpd --listen 0.0.0.0:4317 --data /data/mcpd.db --retention-days 30 --ingest-key $KEY --log-format json"
  />

  <h2>What the SDKs read</h2>
  <p>
    These variables are read by your agent's process, not by the daemon. They
    are listed here because the two sets share names, and a container running
    both an agent and mcpd is configured by one environment.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Variable</th>
          <th>Default</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_ENDPOINT</code></td><td><code>http://localhost:4317</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_API_KEY</code></td><td>unset</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_PROJECT</code></td><td><code>default</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_ENVIRONMENT</code></td><td>unset</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_SERVICE_NAME</code></td><td>unset</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_SERVICE_VERSION</code></td><td>unset</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_SAMPLE</code></td><td><code>1.0</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_ENABLED</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_CAPTURE_PAYLOADS</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MCPD_LOG_LEVEL</code></td><td><code>debug</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <code>MCPD_PROJECT</code> and <code>MCPD_SAMPLE</code> mean the same thing
    on both sides, which is usually what you want: set them once and the agent
    and the daemon agree on the project. Sampling is the one to think about.
    An SDK drops a trace before it is sent, which saves the network. The daemon
    drops it before it is stored, which is the only place the rate can be
    enforced on senders you do not control. Set the rate in one place, not
    both, or you are filtering an already filtered stream.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>MCPD_ENABLED=false</code> makes every SDK call a no-op that still
    returns valid objects, so mcpd can be switched off in tests without
    changing any code. <code>MCPD_CAPTURE_PAYLOADS=false</code> keeps prompts
    and completions in the process: spans still arrive with their timing,
    usage, model and errors, but <code>input</code> and <code>output</code>
    are dropped before they are queued, so they never cross the network.
    Redaction on the daemon is the other end of the same wire, and the wrong
    end for a team that must not send the bytes at all. Per-language detail
    is on each <a href="/docs/sdks/typescript" class="link">SDK page</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>--config-file</h3>
  <p>
    A JSON file holding the settings that do not fit on a command line. It
    takes eleven keys: <code>monitors</code>, <code>slos</code>,
    <code>monitor_interval_seconds</code>, <code>forward</code>,
    <code>judge</code>, <code>projects</code>, <code>runs</code>,
    <code>max_body_bytes</code>, <code>max_batch_signals</code>,
    <code>derived_metrics</code> and <code>aspects</code>. Every one is
    optional. JSON rather than YAML or TOML because mcpd
    already parses JSON for everything else, so the file adds no dependency and
    no second syntax to learn. It mounts as a Kubernetes ConfigMap unchanged.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>max_body_bytes</code> and <code>max_batch_signals</code> are here as
    well as on the command line, so a fleet can keep them beside the monitors
    they are deployed with. A flag beats the file, which is how one instance
    takes a different bound without the shared file being edited for everyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>monitor_interval_seconds</code> is how often the monitors and the
    objectives are evaluated. It defaults to 60, and a value under 5 is raised
    to 5.
  </p>
  <p>
    An unknown key fails the start rather than being ignored, because a typo
    that silently watches nothing is the worst outcome available.
  </p>

  <p>
    So does a value a background timer cannot run with: an
    <code>objective</code> that is not at least 0 and below 1, an objective's
    <code>window_days</code> outside 1 to 365,
    <code>monitor_interval_seconds</code> over 86400,
    <code>aspects.window_seconds</code> outside 1 to 31536000,
    <code>aspects.interval_seconds</code> over 604800, and
    <code>judge.timeout_seconds</code> outside 1 to 300. Each refusal names the
    key and the range.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "max_body_bytes": 33554432,
  "max_batch_signals": 10000,
  "monitor_interval_seconds": 60,
  "monitors": [
    {
      "id": "error-rate",
      "name": "Error rate above 5%",
      "project": "checkout",
      "watch": "error_rate",
      "compare": "above",
      "threshold": 0.05,
      "window_seconds": 300,
      "min_samples": 20,
      "webhook": "https://hooks.example.com/mcpd"
    }
  ],
  "forward": [
    {
      "endpoint": "https://otlp.example.com",
      "headers": [["Authorization", "Bearer sk-..."]],
      "batch_size": 256,
      "enabled": true
    },
    {
      "vendor": "langfuse",
      "public_key": "\${LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY}",
      "secret_key": "\${LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY}"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>forward</code> takes one destination as an object or several as an
    array. Any string in it may be written <code>$&#123;ENV_NAME&#125;</code>
    and is read from the environment at start; an unset variable refuses the
    start and names it. Every field is on the
    <a href="/docs/forwarding" class="link">Forwarding</a> page.
  </p>

  <h3>Projects, and the keys that reach them</h3>
  <p>
    A project is the tenancy key. Listing keys under one is what makes that
    true rather than decorative: a key in a project's block reaches that project
    and no other, so one daemon serves several teams without any of them being
    able to read the others' prompts.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "projects": {
    "checkout": {
      "ingest_keys": ["ck-write-..."],
      "read_keys": ["ck-read-..."],
      "admin_keys": ["ck-admin-..."],
      "retention_days": 30,
      "sample": 0.25
    },
    "support": {
      "ingest_keys": ["sp-write-..."],
      "read_keys": ["sp-read-..."],
      "retention_days": 90
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    All six lists exist per project, as <code>ingest_keys</code>,
    <code>read_keys</code>, <code>write_keys</code>, <code>admin_keys</code>,
    <code>execute_keys</code> and <code>worker_keys</code>, and they fall back
    the same way: a project with no <code>write_keys</code> accepts its own
    ingest keys, one with no <code>admin_keys</code> accepts its own read keys,
    and <code>execute_keys</code> and <code>worker_keys</code> accept the
    project's write keys when they are unset. The
    <code>support</code> block above sets neither, so it behaves exactly as it
    did before the two scopes existed.
  </p>
  <p>
    A key set with any of the six top-level flags reaches every project. That is
    the single-tenant path, and it stays the simple one: if you never open this
    block you never meet any of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>retention_days</code> and <code>sample</code> fall back to the global
    flags when a project does not set them. Both are per project for the same
    reason the keys are: one team's ninety-day requirement should not force
    everybody else's data to be kept, and one team's traffic should not decide
    how another team is sampled.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>tail_sampling</code> keeps a project&rsquo;s finished runs by what
    happened in them and deletes the rest. It takes <code>rate</code>, the
    share of ordinary runs to keep, and keeps every run that matches
    <code>keep_errors</code> (on by default), <code>keep_slower_than_ms</code>,
    <code>keep_cost_above</code>, <code>keep_score_below</code> or
    <code>keep_feedback</code> (on by default). A run is decided
    <code>decide_after_seconds</code> after it finishes, 120 by default, so live
    scores have arrived. <code>mcpd config</code> prints each rule on its
    <code>tail sampling</code> line. See <a href="/docs/sampling">Sampling</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A batch may name several projects. A scoped ingest key writes the signals it
    reaches and refuses the rest, reporting the refusal in the response rather
    than dropping them silently, so a fleet pointed at the wrong project is
    visible from the reply it is already reading.
  </p>
  <p>
    A read request for a project your key does not reach is refused without
    saying whether that project exists. On a shared daemon the project names are
    often the names of teams or customers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Monitors and service level objectives are covered in
    <a href="/docs/alerting" class="link">Monitors and alerts</a>; objectives go
    in an <code>slos</code> array beside <code>monitors</code> and are evaluated
    on the same timer. The
    <code>judge</code> block turns on model-graded scoring and is covered in
    <a href="/docs/testing" class="link">Regression testing</a>. It is off until
    you set it, and a judged scorer never runs unless a request names it,
    because grading spends money on every run it reads.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "judge": {
    "endpoint": "http://localhost:11434/v1",
    "model": "qwen2.5:14b",
    "api_key": "",
    "criteria": {
      "answer_relevance": "Does the answer address the question that was asked?"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <h3>derived_metrics</h3>
  <p>
    Which metrics the daemon derives from the spans it stores. Both halves are
    on when the block is absent. <code>durations</code> writes one duration
    point for every finished agent, tool, workflow, model-generation or
    processor span. <code>tokens</code> writes up to thirteen token counters for
    every model-generation span that reported usage, and only the lines the
    provider reported. Set either to <code>false</code> to stop writing it; a
    field left out stays on, so <code>{'{'}"tokens": false{'}'}</code> keeps the
    durations. There is no flag for this.
  </p>
  <p>
    Derived points are added after the per-project ceiling, so they never cost
    a sender a signal it sent, and they are not counted in the
    <code>accepted</code> number an ingest response reports.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "derived_metrics": { "durations": true, "tokens": false }
}`}</code></pre>

  <h3>aspects</h3>
  <p>
    Whether the daemon clusters aspect notes into themes, and the bounds it does
    it under. Off when the block is absent: a daemon nobody has configured
    starts no analysis timer and writes none of the analysis tables.
    <code>enabled</code> turns the pass on, and needs no model and no
    credential, because the daemon writes the <code>outcome</code> aspect itself
    from facts it already holds. <code>interval_seconds</code> is how often a
    pass runs, 300 by default, and a value under 30 is raised to 30.
    <code>window_seconds</code> is how far back one pass reads notes, 86400 by
    default. <code>disabled</code> names aspects no pass clusters; notes already
    recorded under one stay readable. A storage backend that does not hold the
    analysis tables logs a warning at start and clusters nothing. See
    <a href="/docs/themes" class="link">Themes and snapshots</a>.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "aspects": {
    "enabled": true,
    "interval_seconds": 300,
    "window_seconds": 86400,
    "disabled": ["sentiment"]
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <h3>--forward-endpoint</h3>
  <p>
    Copy what mcpd stores on to another endpoint. Give the base URL;
    <code>/v1/traces</code>, <code>/v1/logs</code> and <code>/v1/metrics</code>
    are appended. The local write always happens first, so a destination that
    is down costs you nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    A destination given by flags replaces the <code>forward</code> block in the
    config file, so one instance can be pointed elsewhere without editing a
    shared ConfigMap. Several destinations at once go in the file. See
    <a href="/docs/forwarding" class="link">Forwarding</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>--forward-header</h3>
  <p>
    A header sent with every forwarded batch, written as
    <code>Name: value</code>. Repeat the flag for more than one. The environment
    variable takes a comma-separated list.
  </p>
  <p>
    A value with no colon is refused at start-up, with the form that works,
    rather than dropped. An OpenTelemetry-style <code>key=value</code> header is
    written <code>key: value</code> here.
  </p>

  <h3>--forward-vendor and --forward-protocol</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--forward-vendor</code> picks a vendor preset, such as
    <code>langfuse</code>, <code>datadog</code> or <code>honeycomb</code>, for
    its endpoint and its attribute conventions. The credential goes in
    <code>--forward-header</code>. With a vendor that has a default endpoint,
    <code>--forward-endpoint</code> may be left out.
    <code>--forward-protocol</code> is <code>json</code>, <code>protobuf</code>
    or <code>grpc</code>, and defaults to the vendor's own. The
    <a href="/docs/forwarding" class="link">Forwarding</a> page lists every
    vendor.
  </p>

  <h3>--spool-dir and --spool-max-bytes</h3>
  <p>
    Where forwarding and <code>mcpd relay</code> keep their spools, one
    directory per destination. It defaults to a <code>.spool</code> directory
    beside <code>--data</code>, such as <code>/data/mcpd.spool</code>, and to
    memory when <code>--data</code> is <code>:memory:</code>. The value
    <code>memory</code> keeps the spools in memory on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--spool-max-bytes</code> is the most one spool may hold:
    268435456 (256 MiB) for each forwarding destination and 1073741824 (1 GiB)
    for the relay unless you set it. A destination's own
    <code>spool_max_bytes</code> in the config file beats it.
  </p>

  <h3>--upstream and --upstream-key</h3>
  <p>
    The daemons <code>mcpd relay</code> ships to, in order of preference.
    Repeat the flag, or give a comma-separated list in
    <code>MCPD_UPSTREAMS</code>. The first upstream that is not cooling off
    after a failure gets each request.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--upstream-key</code> is the key the relay presents to its upstreams,
    as a Bearer token. Unset, each sender's own key is kept with its request
    and passed on. See <a href="/docs/relay" class="link">Relay and regions</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>--clickhouse-url</h3>
  <p>
    The HTTP endpoint of a ClickHouse server, such as
    <code>http://clickhouse:8123</code>. Setting it switches the store from the
    embedded file to ClickHouse, and <code>--data</code> stops being read. The
    schema is created on start.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the setting that removes the single-writer and single-replica limits
    on the embedded store. Reach for it when retention is measured in months of
    production traffic, and not before:
    <a href="/docs/operations" class="link">Running it</a> covers the trade.
  </p>

  <h3>--clickhouse-database, --clickhouse-user, --clickhouse-password</h3>
  <p>
    The database to create tables in, and the credentials to reach it with. A
    user with no password is sent without one, which is right for a container on
    a private network and wrong anywhere else.
  </p>

  <h3>--postgres-url</h3>
  <p>
    A Postgres connection string, such as
    <code>postgres://mcpd:secret@db:5432/mcpd</code>. Setting it switches the
    store from the embedded file to Postgres, and <code>--data</code> stops
    being read. The schema is created on start.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the middle option. It removes the same single-writer and
    single-replica limits ClickHouse does, without asking you to run a database
    you do not already have. Naming both this and
    <code>--clickhouse-url</code> fails the start rather than picking one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Migration takes an advisory lock, so several replicas starting together is
    safe, and a start with nothing to apply touches no schema at all.
  </p>

  <p>
    For TLS, add <code>sslmode=require</code> to the connection string. mcpd then
    encrypts the connection and always verifies the server's certificate. It
    checks it against the public certificate authorities, or against the one in
    <code>sslrootcert=/path/to/ca.pem</code>, which is what a managed service with
    its own authority needs. <code>verify-ca</code> and <code>verify-full</code>
    are read as <code>require</code>. With <code>prefer</code>, the default, or
    <code>disable</code>, the connection is not encrypted.
  </p>

  <h2>Recording who, not just which key</h2>
  <p>
    The audit log records a digest of the key that acted. That answers "an admin
    key deleted this run". For "which person did", mcpd takes the identity from
    the ingress already in front of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Give it the header your proxy sets, and the addresses allowed to set it:
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="take the identity from an ingress you trust"
    command={`mcpd serve \\
  --trusted-identity-header x-forwarded-user \\
  --trusted-proxy-cidr 10.42.0.0/16`}
  />

  <p>
    Both halves are required, and the address list is the reason this is safe. A
    header is a string that anyone able to open a socket to the port can send,
    so mcpd reads it only from an address you have named. A request from
    anywhere else is served normally and its audit row carries no subject rather
    than a guessed one. The value is also length-capped
    and stripped of control characters, because a header is attacker-influenced
    even when the proxy is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    With neither flag set, nothing changes and no subject is ever recorded. That
    is the default.
  </p>

  <h2 id="signup">Open sign-up</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--signup</code> lets anyone create an account, from the interface or
    with <code>POST /v1/api/account/signup</code>. This is how the hosted mcpd
    runs. Sign-up answers the same way whether or not the address is in use and
    mails a link; opening it confirms the address and logs in. Each account gets
    an organisation of its own with a project with a random name, and makes its
    own API keys, one scope per key. A session can do what the account's role
    allows on each project of its organisations; a key can do what its scope
    says on its one project. Neither reaches a project of an organisation the
    account does not belong to, and a span an account key sends is stored in the
    key's project whatever project the span names. See
    <a href="/docs/accounts">accounts and organisations</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turning it on changes one rule for everybody: a request with no key is
    refused, even where no key is configured. Only <code>/healthz</code>,
    <code>/readyz</code>, <code>/metrics</code>, sign-up and log-in stay open.
    Keys you configure keep the reach they have, and are tried first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Accounts live in their own SQLite file, set with
    <code>--accounts-data</code>, whatever stores the signals. The file holds
    argon2id password hashes and SHA-256 digests of tokens and keys, never the
    secrets. Back it up with the rest of the volume.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sign-up is limited to 5 per hour and log-in to 20 attempts per 15 minutes
    for each client address, and failed log-ins to 10 per 15 minutes for each
    email. Behind a proxy every connection comes from the proxy, so name the
    header it puts the client's address in, and the addresses the proxy
    connects from:
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="open sign-up behind Cloudflare and a proxy on a private network"
    command={`mcpd serve --signup \\
  --client-ip-header cf-connecting-ip \\
  --trusted-proxy-cidr 172.16.0.0/12 \\
  --cors-origin https://mcpd.sh`}
  />

  <p>
    The header is read only from an address in <code>--trusted-proxy-cidr</code>.
    From a trusted proxy that did not set it, the first address in
    <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> is used. From any other address, the peer
    address is the client, whatever the headers say. Make sure only your proxy
    can reach the daemon: a client that reaches it from inside a trusted range
    can name any address it likes. <code>--cors-origin</code> is needed when the
    interface is served from another origin than the daemon.
  </p>


  <h2 id="accounts">Accounts without sign-up</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--accounts</code> turns on the same accounts, organisations, roles,
    single sign-on and SCIM without public sign-up, for a deployment on your
    own cluster. People arrive by invitation, by single sign-on or by SCIM, and
    configured keys keep working beside them. As with <code>--signup</code>, a
    request with no credential is refused.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mail goes through <code>--smtp-host</code>, with <code>--smtp-from</code>,
    <code>--smtp-port</code>, <code>--smtp-user</code>,
    <code>--smtp-password</code> and <code>--smtp-tls</code>. With no SMTP
    server the links are written to the log at info level, except with
    <code>--signup</code> on a public address, where sign-up, password reset,
    address changes and invitations answer 503 and the daemon warns at start.
    Links and the single sign-on redirect start at <code>--public-url</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--org-quota-per-minute</code> is the signals-per-minute ceiling an
    organisation shares across its projects, and the most it may set for
    itself. <code>--org-retention-days</code> is how long its projects keep
    data, and the longest it may choose.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <code>accounts</code> block of the config file holds
    <code>allow_org_creation</code>, <code>allow_private_issuers</code> and
    <code>orgs</code>. Each entry of <code>orgs</code> declares an organisation
    by <code>slug</code>, with a <code>name</code>, its <code>owners</code> by
    email, existing <code>projects</code> it owns,
    <code>quota_per_minute</code>, <code>retention_days</code>,
    <code>group_roles</code>, <code>scim_token_env</code> and
    <code>sso</code>. The <code>sso</code> block takes <code>provider</code>
    (<code>oidc</code> or <code>github</code>), <code>issuer</code>,
    <code>client_id</code>, <code>client_secret_env</code>,
    <code>domains</code>, <code>enforce</code>, <code>default_role</code> and
    <code>groups_claim</code>. Secrets are read from the environment variables
    the file names, never from the file.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="single sign-on and roles on your own cluster"
    command={`mcpd serve --accounts --config-file mcpd.json \\
  --public-url https://mcpd.example.com \\
  --smtp-host smtp.example.com --smtp-from no-reply@example.com`}
  />

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "accounts": {
    "orgs": [{
      "slug": "acme",
      "name": "Acme",
      "owners": ["ana@acme.com"],
      "projects": ["default"],
      "sso": {
        "issuer": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/TENANT/v2.0",
        "client_id": "CLIENT_ID",
        "client_secret_env": "MCPD_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET",
        "domains": ["acme.com"],
        "enforce": true,
        "default_role": "member",
        "groups_claim": "groups"
      },
      "group_roles": { "mcpd-admins": "admin", "mcpd-viewers": "viewer" },
      "scim_token_env": "MCPD_SCIM_TOKEN"
    }]
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>One project cannot spend the whole daemon</h2>
  <p>
    A project that floods mcpd costs every other project on it: the disk fills,
    retention cannot keep up, and the team that did nothing wrong is the one
    that loses runs. <code>--quota-per-minute</code> is the ceiling that stops
    that.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="a ceiling for every project, and a larger one for one of them"
    command="mcpd serve --quota-per-minute 10000"
  />

  <p>
    Unset means no ceiling, so a deployment that never sets it behaves exactly
    as it did before quotas existed. Set, it covers three things: every project
    in the <code>projects</code> block that does not name its own
    <code>quota_per_minute</code>, the project named by <code>--project</code>,
    and <strong>every project the configuration does not list at all</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last one is the part that makes it a ceiling rather than a suggestion.
    The project name arrives inside the request body, so a sender that picks a
    name no configuration file lists used to be charged to nothing and could
    store without bound. A limit the sender can leave by inventing a string is
    not a limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every unlisted project shares one counter rather than getting one each. A
    counter per name would be an allocation any sender could drive by inventing
    names, which is the same hole in a different shape. Sharing means a flood
    spread over a thousand invented names is bounded in total by this number,
    and it means a project worth its own allowance should be named in
    <code>projects</code>, where it gets one.
  </p>

  <h3>What the ceiling actually bounds</h3>
  <p>
    The counter is the two-window weighted kind: this minute's count, plus the
    minute before it weighted by how much of it the window ending now still
    covers. It holds the sustained rate to the ceiling and permits a burst of up
    to a whole ceiling on top of that, because an SDK flushing a full queue
    arrives as one burst and a counter that refused it would refuse ordinary
    traffic.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those two together have an arithmetic consequence worth stating rather than
    hiding. A project may store a shade under <strong>twice</strong> its ceiling
    inside one 60-second span: a full ceiling at the very end of one window, and
    a full ceiling again at the very end of the next. That is not an artefact of
    the approximation. Any limiter that lets a project spend its ceiling at once
    and its ceiling again per minute afterwards permits that across a boundary,
    and the only way to remove it is to refuse the burst.
  </p>
  <p>
    The window is one minute and is not configurable. The flag names the unit,
    so a second setting that changed what "per minute" meant would make the
    first number unreadable.
  </p>

  <h3>What happens to the excess</h3>
  <p>
    Signals over the ceiling are refused and reported, never dropped in silence.
    The batch is not refused whole: mcpd stores what fits and tells you what did
    not.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Spans go by whole trace. Half a run has spans whose parent nobody stored,
      which reads as a broken run rather than a missing one. A trace too large
      for what is left is skipped and the next one tried, so one big run does
      not spend the remainder of the allowance on nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Logs, metrics, scores and feedback stand alone, so they fill whatever is
      left in arrival order.
    </li>
    <li>
      Only what is stored is charged. The trim keeps whole traces and so can
      store fewer signals than it was permitted, and the difference is handed
      back. Without that, a project whose runs are larger than its remaining
      allowance would pay its way to zero while storing nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Other projects in the same request are untouched. One project over its
      ceiling does not cost another the signals it sent alongside.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The reply carries the count in <code>rejected</code> and a line in
    <code>errors</code> naming the project and its ceiling. A project the
    configuration never listed gets a different sentence, because being told
    that project <code>analytics-v2</code> is over its ceiling sends you looking
    for a setting that does not exist when the answer is to add the project:
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="eight signals into a project nobody configured, with the shared ceiling at five"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -sX POST "$MCPD/v1/ingest" -H content-type:application/json -d @batch.json | jq' },
      { out: '{' },
      { out: '  "accepted": 5,' },
      { out: '  "rejected": 3,', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  "duplicate": 0,' },
      { out: '  "errors": [' },
      { out: '    {' },
      { out: '      "signal": "span",' },
      { out: '      "index": 0,' },
      { out: '      "message": "3 signal(s) named project analytics-v2, which', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '                  no configuration lists. Every unlisted project', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '                  shares one ceiling of 5 per minute and it was', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '                  full, so they were not stored. Add analytics-v2', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '                  to the `projects` block to give it an allowance', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '                  of its own, or raise --quota-per-minute."', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '    }' },
      { out: '  ]' },
      { out: '}' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    A refusal is counted in <code>mcpd_signals_over_quota_total</code> as well
    as in <code>mcpd_signals_rejected_total</code>, so a fleet being limited is
    distinguishable from a fleet sending malformed data. See
    <a href="/docs/operations" class="link">Running it</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Two things to check when you set it</h3>
  <p>
    The ceiling covers every door that stores a signal: the batch ones,
    <code>/v1/ingest</code>, <code>/v1/traces</code>, <code>/v1/logs</code> and
    <code>/v1/metrics</code>, and the single-record ones,
    <code>POST /v1/api/scores</code> and <code>POST /v1/api/feedback</code>.
    The two answer a project over its ceiling differently, on purpose. A batch
    door stores what fits and reports the rest as <code>rejected</code> with a
    reason, because a batch may name several projects and refusing the whole
    request would punish the ones inside their ceiling. A single-record door
    has no partial answer to give, so it refuses with <code>429</code> and a
    <code>Retry-After</code> header carrying the seconds until that project's
    counter admits another signal. The number comes from the counter itself, so
    a client that honours it comes back when there is room rather than when a
    fixed delay is up.
  </p>
  <p>
    One write door is not charged to it: <code>POST /v1/api/score</code>, which
    runs scorers over stored runs. It hands the scores it produced straight to
    storage, so a pass lands on disk for a project whose ceiling is already
    spent, and those rows are counted by neither
    <code>mcpd_signals_over_quota_total</code> nor
    <code>mcpd_signals_unbounded_total</code>. It is a door you open yourself
    rather than one a fleet points at, so treat the ceiling as a bound on what
    senders can push in, not on everything that can reach the disk.
  </p>
  <p>
    A ceiling below <code>--max-batch-signals</code>, 10,000 by default, means
    one legal batch can exceed a whole minute's allowance and is trimmed
    however well behaved the sender is. <code>mcpd doctor</code> grades this under
    <code>limits</code> and names the projects it applies to. It also warns when
    a ceiling is set per project and no top-level one is set beside it, which is
    the configuration that leaves every unlisted project unbounded.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd config</code> prints the limited projects by name, so you can
    read back what the setting covers rather than inferring it. It prints the
    named ceilings only, so confirm the shared one from the flag itself.
  </p>

</Doc>
